REC² - COCORES collaboration map concept note
Proposal for the project team
We propose a living, searchable map that shows how the six RAP themes, six case studies, teams, researchers, and publications connect. 
The first version would help colleagues find relevant work and contact one another. 
With agreed data and research governance, later versions could examine how these connections change over time.

[Risks and food for thought: DMP and what could leak? Shall I keep it public for now?]

What colleagues would see

The opening screen shows REUSE, ROT, REPLACE, REDUCE, RECYCLE and RESPECT in the project palette, alongside Case Studies 1–6.
Selecting a theme or case study reveals its participating teams and people. 
A person card shows their team, case studies, and chosen contact details. 
Users can explore publications by author and keyword. 
A time control can show when recorded relationships began or ended.

How the information fits together

Themes and case studies have many-to-many links. 
A team contributes to at least one theme; each person belongs to one team and works on at least one case study.
A publication can have multiple authors and keywords. 
Authorship supplies a clear, attributable basis for showing collaboration; shared membership or shared case-study work can be displayed separately. 
Links may carry start and end dates, and the interface should distinguish unknown dates from absent links.

First release and research use
For a pitch, we would show an interactive example with the SW group [What about Erbe and Dornack?]. 
After participating teams check their entries, an initial live release could include names, affiliations, project involvement, and contact preferences. 
Publications and dated relationships can follow as records become available.
Research use would require a separately defined question, data provenance, and an interpretation protocol;
a network connection by itself does not establish the quality or intensity of collaboration.

Proposed participation and next decision
We would ask each team to identify a contact who can review the case study and theme links, 
then invite individuals to check their own profile and contact visibility.[Before collecting or publishing personal data, 
the project should agree on who may view and edit it, how corrections work, 
and whether any research analysis needs separate consent or review.] 
The immediate decision requested is whether to pilot this map with a small set of 
project members and validate the structure before wider collection.

